// x-recursion: a recursion whose only stop is a bottom argument never stops,
// and nothing says so
//
// `walk(n.next$)` reads as "recurse while there is a next node". The call is
// an activation whether or not its argument has a value, and that activation
// makes the same call, so the recursion has no end: the node-walk binds
// activations until the budget (or memory) stops it, and the fused tail loop
// spins in native code, counted by no budget, printing nothing until the
// interrupt. `--check` accepts the program with no warning. The same holds
// for an argument gated by `filter` or `never()`, in the program's first
// cycle and in an arm entered later.
//
// command:
//   GRAPHIX_ACTIVATION_BUDGET=30000 timeout -s KILL 20 \
//     /home/eric/tmp/review-2026-11-16/bin/graphix --no-cache --no-fusion \
//     design/review-2026-11-16/repro/x-recursion-bottom-ended-recursion.gx
//   GRAPHIX_ACTIVATION_BUDGET=30000 timeout -s KILL 20 \
//     /home/eric/tmp/review-2026-11-16/bin/graphix --no-cache \
//     design/review-2026-11-16/repro/x-recursion-bottom-ended-recursion.gx
//
// expected: visit 1, visit 2, visit 3, then exit 0; or a check-time warning
//   that the self-call is outside every select arm and cannot stop.
// observed, --no-fusion: visit 1..3, then
//   Error: a runaway recursion exceeded the runtime's budget ... (exit 1;
//   without a budget it grows until memory runs out)
// observed, fusion on: no output, 100% CPU until the timeout kills it
//   (exit 137); SIGQUIT ends it with status 130 and only then are the three
//   visits printed.
type N = {v: i64, next: [N, null]};
let rec walk = |n: N| -> null { println("visit [n.v]"); walk(n.next$) };
let l: N = {v: 1, next: {v: 2, next: {v: 3, next: null}}};
sys::exit(sys::time::after_idle(duration:500.ms, 0));
walk(l)
